<Project>
  <!--
    ============================================================================================
    SHARED MOD ASSET GLOBS. The mod asset trees live at the repo root (../assets/<domain>/), hoisted
    out of each src/<Mod>/ so non-code contributors (translators, shape and texture artists) have a
    single top-level assets/ view and never touch src/. It is a pure MSBuild relocation - invisible to
    the game, which only cares about the assets/<domain>/… layout INSIDE the packaged mod.

    Here rather than in Directory.Build.props because the glob list is a PROPERTY computed from
    $(AssetDomain), and properties in that file are evaluated before the csproj assigns it: the
    condition would read empty, nothing would be packed, and nothing would error. This file is
    imported after the csproj, so the value is there. (Plain item groups keyed on $(AssetDomain) are
    fine in props - items are evaluated after every property from both files.)

    A mod packs its primary domain plus, optionally, further trees:
      * $(AssetDomainsExtra) - semicolon-separated. A mod ships more than one while absorbing another
        mod's codes during a merge, because the absorbed tree keeps shipping under its own domain
        until the codes relocate.
      * $(ShipGameLangOverride) - the spelling retained for the assets/game/ lang override that
        renames vanilla keys (iron -> "wrought iron"). Only ONE mod may opt in or the override ships
        duplicated; today that is iiex. It is now this same mechanism, not a separate glob.

    Wildcards are expanded from a property, never from an item transform: MSBuild expands them in a
    literal Include, but a value produced by a transform stays literal, and the copy then fails on a
    path containing "**\*".

    Link is derived from each file's own path rather than from the domain, so one glob serves every
    tree. Without it MSBuild flattens these out-of-cone files to the output root instead of
    assets/<domain>/….
    ============================================================================================
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(AssetDomain)' != ''">
    <_ExAssetsRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\assets</_ExAssetsRoot>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    One literal glob per tree. Deliberately repetitive rather than batched over a domain list: a
    wildcard is only expanded in a literal Include, so every shorter spelling fails, and each fails in
    a way that looks like it worked.
      * @(domains->'…\%(Identity)\**\*') - a value from an item transform is never wildcard-expanded,
        so the literal "**\*" reaches the copy and it fails on a file that does not exist.
      * that transform built into a property first - item references are not expanded in properties
        during evaluation at all; the property just holds the "@(…)" text.
      * a batched target with an unqualified %(RecursiveDir) in Link - the metadata resolves against
        whatever else is in scope, which silently re-linked the project's OWN modicon and modinfo under
        assets/<domain>/ and packed no asset files at all.

    Primary + game + two absorbed trees covers what a merge needs (an absorbing mod packs its own tree,
    the absorbed one until its codes relocate, and the game-domain lang override). Add a third property
    here if that ever stops being enough - three lines beats a mechanism that keeps biting.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(AssetDomain)' != ''">
    <Content Include="$(_ExAssetsRoot)\$(AssetDomain)\**\*">
      <Link>assets\$(AssetDomain)\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>

    <!-- The assets/game/ lang override that renames vanilla keys (iron -> "wrought iron"). Only ONE
         mod may opt in or the override ships duplicated; today that is iiex. -->
    <Content
      Condition="'$(ShipGameLangOverride)' == 'true'"
      Include="$(_ExAssetsRoot)\game\**\*"
    >
      <Link>assets\game\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>

    <Content
      Condition="'$(AssetDomainAbsorbed1)' != ''"
      Include="$(_ExAssetsRoot)\$(AssetDomainAbsorbed1)\**\*"
    >
      <Link>assets\$(AssetDomainAbsorbed1)\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>

    <Content
      Condition="'$(AssetDomainAbsorbed2)' != ''"
      Include="$(_ExAssetsRoot)\$(AssetDomainAbsorbed2)\**\*"
    >
      <Link>assets\$(AssetDomainAbsorbed2)\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
